# Playbooks

Every playbook ends the same way: `bun run fix`, then `bun run check`, all green, then commit.

## Add a config option or generator feature

1. Write the failing test first, in the file of the layer that owns the behavior
   (`src/core/<generator>/index.test.ts` for output, `src/config/index.test.ts` for validation).
2. Add the field to `ConfigSchema` in `src/config/index.ts`, with its default as
   `Schema.withDecodingDefault` and a check whose message names the field.
3. Thread it through `makeJob` in `src/shared/index.ts`, so the CLI and the Vite plugin both
   get it. Never special-case one entry point.
4. Implement it in `core/` (the Effect program) and `generator/` / `helper/` (the pure code
   builders), respecting the layering.
5. If the output is a client hook, add or update a case under `test/cases/` and assert it in
   `test/runtime/` or `test/types/`.
6. Document it in `packages/asphodelos/README.md`: the section of that generator and
   `Full Config Reference`.

## Fix a generator bug

1. Reproduce it as a failing test at the smallest document that shows it. Prefer asserting the
   behavior of the generated code (`src/core/runtime.test.ts` mounts the real Elysia app) over
   string matching.
2. Find the layer that owns the mistake — parsing (`openapi/`), a code builder (`generator/`),
   composition (`helper/`), or the program (`core/`) — and fix it there, not downstream.
3. Check whether sibling generators or the other client libraries have the same mistake.
4. If the fix changes generated output, run `bun run test:clients` and read what changed.

## Port a feature from hono-takibi

1. Read the hono-takibi implementation and its tests end to end before writing anything.
2. List what differs here: Elysia instead of Hono, `elysia` / `test` output that merges into the
   user's code, bun instead of pnpm / vitest, this repository's lint rules. Adapt; do not copy
   a behavior that would delete or overwrite user code.
3. Port the tests first, translated to `bun:test`, and make sure the new ones fail against the
   current code before the implementation lands.
4. Say in the commit body what was taken as-is and what was changed and why.

## Support a vendor extension (`x-*`)

1. Add it to the model in `src/openapi/vendor-ext.ts`.
2. Emit it from the TypeBox builder (`src/generator/typebox/`), keeping the keyword on the
   emitted schema so it round-trips.
3. Prove it against a real request in `src/core/runtime.test.ts`: the status and, for message
   extensions, the exact text. If Elysia cannot honor it, test that it survives on the schema and
   document it as round-trip only.
4. Add it to the README `Vendor Extensions` tables.

## Clear a lint failure

1. Run the failing command and read the rule name in the message.
2. Try `bun run fix` first — formatting, import order, markdownlint and textlint are autofixable.
3. Otherwise **change the code, not the rule.** The rules encode the design: `no-let` means one
   const expression; no assertion means narrow with a guard; a `no-restricted-imports` message
   means the dependency direction is wrong; `prefer-each` means `it.each`.
4. Only if the rule genuinely cannot hold, add
   `// oxlint-disable-next-line rule-name -- reason` on that line, and say in the report that
   you added a suppression and why.
5. An unknown word is one line in `project-words.txt` if it is a real project term, a file-local
   `cspell:ignore` if it is test data, and a rename if it is an abbreviation or a typo.

## Add a lint rule

1. Check `oxlint --rules` for its category; correctness / suspicious / perf are already on.
2. Add it to `packages/asphodelos/oxlint.config.ts` in its section, with the reason.
3. Run `bun run lint` and `bun run test:clients` (which lints `test/`), and fix every finding in
   the code.
4. For a `custom/` rule, follow the plugin section of the CI and configuration rule.

## Bump effect

1. Change `effect`, `@effect/platform-node` and `@effect/platform-node-shared` in
   `packages/asphodelos/package.json` together (all exact), then `bun install`.
2. `bun run typecheck`. The rc moves the Schema and CLI APIs, so expect errors in `config/` and
   `cli/` first.
3. `bun run check`. Read every behavior difference rather than adjusting an assertion to match.
   `test:pack` failing on "exactly one copy of effect" means another `@effect/*` package in the
   chain needs pinning to the same version.
4. Report which upstream changes forced code changes; that is what the commit body explains.

## Release (only when asked)

1. Confirm `main` is green.
2. Bump `version` in `packages/asphodelos/package.json` only (semver: a breaking change to the
   config, the CLI or the generated output is a minor bump while the version is `0.x`).
3. `bun run check`, then commit with the version as the subject (`0.1.0`) and push to `main`.
4. Watch Test, then `release`: it tags `v<version>`, publishes to npm and creates the GitHub
   release from that commit. Never publish or tag by hand.
5. If a job fails, fix the cause and re-run the failed jobs. To fix a published release, bump
   again — never reuse a version.
